# Prices in euros at the ferry town's market, and dear and cheap.
lesson language-how-much v=1 format=teach grade=3 unit=4 subject=language levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "[[phrase.how-much-is-it]]"
  goal "Ask how much something costs in [[language.name]] and understand a price in euros."

  look {
    scene 28x14 {
      column all at=canvas(1, 1) space=1 align=start {
        foodstall stall foods=["bread", "cheese", "fish", "egg"] names=["[[food.bread]]", "[[food.cheese]]", "[[food.fish]]", "[[food.eggs]]"] costs=["2 €", "5 €", "7 €", "3 €"]
      }
    }
    say """
      At the market, ask [[phrase.how-much-is-it]], how much does it cost? The answer is a price:
      [[phrase.it-costs-two-euros]], it costs two euros. The ferry town is in Spain, where people
      pay in euros, written with €. Something that costs a lot is [[market.expensive]], and
      something that costs little is [[market.cheap]].
      """
  }
  do {
    level easy {
      worked language.how-much-name k=4
    }
    show language.how-much-name k=2 easy-k=0 hard-k=5
    show language.how-much-meaning k=3 easy-k=2 hard-k=4
    show language.how-much-write k=4 easy-k=2 hard-k=6
    show language.how-much-name k=4 easy-k=1 hard-k=6
    show language.how-much-match k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
    show language.how-much-write k=5 easy-k=4 hard-k=3
    show language.how-much-listen k=3 easy-k=0 hard-k=5
    show language.how-much-say k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
    level hard {
      show language.how-much-write k=2
    }
  }
  language es {
    try stars=2 {
      show language.es-how-much-why k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
    }
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show language.how-much-puzzle k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
  }
  remember {
    say "[[phrase.how-much-is-it]] How much is it? [[phrase.it-costs-two-euros]] It costs two euros."
  }

  grown-ups """
    The wrong idea to watch for is a price heard as the teen it sounds like: two and twelve, three
    and thirteen, five and fifteen, which every naming question offers side by side. Say both aloud
    and ask which the card shows. Prices are in euros because the ferry town is in Spain; the
    stall's cards show the digits, so the child only turns them into words. The listening question
    is a dictation: read the sentence aloud from this sheet, where it prints as Read aloud, or let
    the device play it. It is marked word by word, and a missing accent counts there, while the
    words typed on their own in this lesson are taken without their accents. If the child is stuck,
    set up a shop at home with price labels, and buy and sell in the language. Off the page, look at
    a price in a shop and say it in the language. It meets England's key stage 2 languages programme
    (years 3 to 6, statutory from age 7): "engage in conversations; ask and answer questions";
    Japan's grades 5 and 6 foreign languages: "Activities to listen to concrete information
    regarding familiar and simple matters in daily life such as dates, times and prices etc."; and
    CEFR A2, "Can understand sentences and frequently used expressions related to areas of most
    immediate relevance (e.g. very basic personal and family information, shopping, local geography,
    employment)". The phrases are in [[language.variety]].
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level [[market.three-euros]] is worked first in the teacher's pen, the words to name are [[market.two-euros]] and [[market.five-euros]] and the words to write are [[num.ten]] and [[num.twelve]], the plainer ones of the lesson, with the first set to match, the shortest sentence to hear and the first version of each star question."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the words to name are [[market.twelve-euros]] and [[market.four-euros]] and the words to write are [[num.fifteen]], [[num.three]] and [[num.ten]], the ones most often mixed up, with the last set to match, the longest sentence to hear, a third word to write and the last version of each star question."
  }
}
